[[0,569497,0,6,1,5,"2015-10-07T05:46:09","scrotums","",[],"","Why Hillary Clinton Feels Safe Running To The Left Of Bernie Sanders On Guns","http://fivethirtyeight.com/datalab/why-clinton-feels-safe-running-to-sanders-left-on-guns/","politics"],[1,2737676,0,1,0,1,"2015-10-07T07:50:26","littlemikemac","",[],"<p>Well that was a creepy ass article. It's not Congresses job to dislodge beliefs through legislation. And it's not all that accurate to critize the Senate as being undemocratic because it doesn't function like the House, the two sectors of Congress were designed to serve two different areas of democracy. The House gives each state a number of seats based on the population so it can better reflect the general public, the Senate gives each state two seats so that a more populus state isn't easily able to pursue its interests in a way that negatively effects a less populous state. </p>\n<p>The real change this article is proposing is to erode our democracy to allow the more populous states to essentially turn less populous states into their colonies. </p>\n<p>Fuck that author.</p>"],[2,2737871,2737676,3,0,3,"2015-10-07T08:13:19","KleanRider","",[],"<p>Yeah 538 is kinda weird. Nate Silver is the only guy that seems to let the numbers dictate the narrative but the other authors manipulate statistics to fit their narrative.</p>"],[3,2738065,2737871,0,0,0,"2015-10-07T08:42:18","littlemikemac","",[],"<p>This is a bit more than manipulating the statistics. This calling for a full on suspension of republicanism (as in the form of government not the party) in less populous states. They want conservative and liberal citizens alike turned into subjects of an Aristocratic industrial state.</p>"]]
